{#-
  Leg 2 — ship. Included by taskdef/release/instructions.txt under its
  "## Leg 2 — ship" heading, so this body starts at H3. The release
  parameters (release.github_target, release.tag_prefix, release.signing_key,
  release.changelog, and the optional release.dep_refresh) were resolved in
  Phase 0.5; leg 2 consumes all but release.changelog. Step names extend the
  derived-step vocabulary of the resume decision table in
  release-resume.jinja2.
-#}
Entry condition: the human merged the release MR (`prep-release` → `main`)
on GitLab — or a relaunch derived a leg-2 step from run state. Everything in
this leg keys on ONE fact: the release-MR merge SHA recorded in run state.
No step acts on an earlier leg's memory, the working tree, or a branch tip
that could move — a step either verifies its target against the recorded
merge SHA or it does not run.

On entry (and on every re-entry) run `work release status` first: it prints
the recorded fields, the derived leg, and the single next step. Record each
step's phase before starting it (`work state set --phase=<step name>`, using
the step names below). Four of these names — `leg2-record-merge`,
`leg2-preconditions`, `leg2-leak-scan` and `leg2-ci-candidate` — extend the
resume decision table's derived-step vocabulary without ever appearing as a
derived next step: `work release status` folds them into its rows. A
derivation of `leg2-create-tag` therefore runs steps 1 through 3: step 1 as
an idempotent re-record (its mismatch check runs every time, even when the
gate already recorded the merge SHA), then step 2's preconditions, step
2a's leak scan and step 2b's GitHub CI candidate, then the tag.

Credentials are provisioned, never fetched: the GitHub fine-grained PAT
arrives through this session's credential env as `LMER_RELEASE_GITHUB_TOKEN`,
and the release SSH signing key is mounted read-only at
`/release-signing-key` (the path is also exported as
`LMER_RELEASE_SIGNING_KEY`); `release.signing_key` is the REFERENCE resolved
in Phase 0.5. Consume these deliveries as-is — do NOT hunt for keys
elsewhere, generate one, or re-implement provisioning. A missing or rejected
credential is a hard error naming the credential requirement (see HARD
RULES), never a workaround.

`release.github_target` is a bare `https://` URL and the container carries no
persistent credential helper for the mirror host, with `~/.gitconfig` a
read-only mount. Every operation against the `github` remote — every fetch,
every `ls-remote` check, and every `bin/gate-push` invocation (the
release-candidate branch, `main`, the tag) — therefore fails
unauthenticated. Supply the PAT through lmer's action-free
ASKPASS helper. The helper only answers Git's credential prompts from its own
environment; it performs no network or filesystem action.

Supply it PER COMMAND, never once for the leg: each command runs in a fresh
shell, so an `export` in one invocation does not reach the next. The prefix
below goes in front of EACH `github`-remote command, in the same shell
invocation as that command — every fetch, `ls-remote` check and
`bin/gate-push` invocation (`bin/gate-push` shells out to `git`, so
ASKPASS reaches Git through the environment it inherits):

```
# `x-access-token` is the fixed username the forge expects for a PAT; the
# provisioned token is the password half. LC_ALL=C makes Git's ASKPASS prompt
# vocabulary match the helper's audited Username/Password contract.
(
  export LC_ALL=C
  export GIT_ASKPASS=/Agents/global/bin/lmer-git-askpass
  export GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0
  export LMER_GIT_ASKPASS_USERNAME=x-access-token
  export LMER_GIT_ASKPASS_PASSWORD="${LMER_RELEASE_GITHUB_TOKEN}"
  <the github-remote command>
)
```

The subshell keeps ASKPASS scoped to the one command that needs it, and
`export` is a builtin, so the token never reaches any process's command line.
Never write the token into the remote URL, a config file, or run state. In
particular, do not use numbered Git config around `bin/gate-push`: the gate
runs pytest in its inherited environment, and tests own their isolated Git
config entries.

### Step 1 — `leg2-record-merge` — record the merge SHA

The anchor for every later step. Skipping it or reordering it breaks the
whole ladder.

- [ ] Read the release MR's merge SHA from GitLab (`gitlab-review` on the
      release MR — the merge commit on `main`). Full 40-hex, never
      abbreviated.
- [ ] Re-read the version AT THAT SHA — never the working tree, never leg
      1's memory: `git fetch origin` then
      `git show <merge-sha>:pyproject.toml`, extracting `project.version`
      from that exact blob.
- [ ] Record both together:
      `work release record merge-sha <merge-sha> --version <observed-version>`

The recorder HARD STOPS when the observed version disagrees with leg 1's
recorded version. That mismatch means a second bump or a foreign commit
landed on `main` — a human decision is required, not a repair attempt.
Record it before ending the session:
`work state set --stop-reason=critical_error --critical-error
'{"summary": "version mismatch at merge SHA", "detail": "leg 1 recorded <recorded> but pyproject.toml at <merge-sha> says <observed>"}'`
then `work commit` and end the session. Re-recording the same SHA and
version on a resume is safe — the mismatch check runs even on an idempotent
re-record.

### Step 2 — `leg2-preconditions` — preconditions, each a receipt

Both checks run through `work verify` so a receipt exists, not just prose:

- [ ] GitLab `main` pipeline at the merge SHA is green. Resolve the
      pipeline FOR the merge SHA first (GitLab API
      `/projects/<id>/pipelines?sha=<merge-sha>` — always pinned to the
      merge SHA, never "latest on main"), then record the receipt:
      `work verify gitlab-main-pipeline -- gitlab-pipeline <project> <pipeline-id> --host <hostname> --expect-status success`
      (`--expect-status success` makes the exit code carry the verdict — a
      red or still-running pipeline exits non-zero, so the receipt can
      never record a green checkmark for a red pipeline.)
      Red or still running → do not proceed; wait or investigate.
- [ ] GitHub `main` is an ancestor of the merge SHA. Add the mirror remote
      once (`git remote add github <release.github_target>` — skip if
      already present), fetch it with the credential prefix above in the
      same shell invocation as the fetch, then:
      `work verify github-ancestry -- git merge-base --is-ancestor <github-main-sha> <merge-sha>`
      where `<github-main-sha>` is `git rev-parse github/main` after the
      fetch.

GitHub `main` holding any commit that is NOT an ancestor of the merge SHA is
divergence — a HARD STOP with a documented remediation path, NEVER a
force-push. Record a critical error naming both SHAs and the fork point
(`git merge-base github/main <merge-sha>`), `work commit`, end the session.
The remediation path is a human one: inspect what landed on the mirror
out-of-band, remove or merge it deliberately on the canonical GitLab side,
and relaunch the release — leg 2 re-derives from run state and re-checks.

### Step 2a — `leg2-leak-scan` — the gating leak scan at the merge SHA

The gate's early scan saw the bump; this one sees exactly what a push of the
merge SHA publishes, the release merge and its message included. It runs on
every entry to this step; it is cheap, and re-running it is how a re-entry
converges.

- [ ] `leak_scan.public_mirror` is `false` → `work log` that the scan and
      the pre-push guard are opted out; go to step 2b.
- [ ] With both remotes freshly fetched in step 2:
      `work verify leak-scan -- lmer-release-guard scan --config <resolved task.yaml> --base <previous tag> --candidate <merge-sha> --published github/main --out <scratch dir>/leak-scan.json`
      where `<previous tag>` is
      `git describe --tags --abbrev=0 --match 'v[0-9]*' github/main`.
- [ ] Triage exactly as the gate's early scan does (exit 1 is a hard stop,
      never zero hits; exit 2 is a finding by definition; every other hit
      is cleared against the exceptions file,
      the published history and recorded operator decisions, or it is a
      finding). Add a "Gating rescan at `<merge-sha>`" section to
      `leak-scan.md` and persist it again with `work artifact`.
- [ ] Any finding → STOP before anything is pushed: `lmer-signal ask
      --operator-only` naming each finding (masked), record the question,
      `work commit`, end the session. Nothing is rewritten without the
      operator's decision.

### Step 2b — `leg2-ci-candidate` — GitHub CI on the exact merge SHA

**No tag, local or remote, until GitHub CI has passed on exactly the
recorded merge SHA.** The tag push runs `release.yml`, whose checks are the
same suite on the same runner image: a candidate that is red here would fail
there after the tag exists, leaving an immutable tag with nothing published.
GitHub's runner image differs from the gate container and GitLab CI (it
ships tools they lack), so this is the one place that class of failure
shows.

Every `gh` command below runs in its own subshell exporting `GH_TOKEN`,
`GH_REPO` and `GH_PROMPT_DISABLED=1`, exactly as step 5 describes; every
`github`-remote git command carries the ASKPASS prefix. Write each finding
of this step to `github-ci-candidate.md` as you go and persist it with
`work artifact`.

1. [ ] **Guard** (skip when opted out): on a fresh credentialed fetch,
       `work verify prepush-guard-candidate -- lmer-release-guard prepush --config <resolved task.yaml> --version <X.Y.Z> --published github/main <merge-sha> refs/heads/release-candidate/v<X.Y.Z>`.
       `GUARD FAIL` → STOP with an operator-only ask quoting its FAIL lines;
       a non-zero exit without a `GUARD FAIL` line is an error, and a stop
       too — the guard did not run.
2. [ ] **Push the candidate** — skip when current:
       `git ls-remote github refs/heads/release-candidate/v<X.Y.Z>` already
       showing the merge SHA. Otherwise:
       `bin/gate-push --ref <merge-sha>:refs/heads/release-candidate/v<X.Y.Z> --remote github`.
       The push allow list must grant that ref on the mirror. A refusal is
       an operator-only ask for the grant, never a different route; an
       operator can grant it for one command (a command-scoped
       `LMER_PUSH_ALLOW_LIST` union) or standing. A non-fast-forward
       rejection (a candidate branch from a superseded run at a commit
       this SHA does not descend from) is an operator-only ask — never a
       force-push.
3. [ ] **Draft PR into `main`** — adopt the open PR whose head is
       `release-candidate/v<X.Y.Z>` if there is one
       (`gh pr list --head release-candidate/v<X.Y.Z> --state open`);
       otherwise
       `gh pr create --draft --base main --head release-candidate/v<X.Y.Z> --title "Release candidate v<X.Y.Z> (CI only, not for merge)" --body "CI evidence for the v<X.Y.Z> release candidate. Not for merge."`
       The PR is public: its title and body carry nothing else. It is CI
       evidence only — NEVER merge it.
4. [ ] **Wait for CI** without watching it in the foreground, in ONE
       background job with one completion callback to this session; yield
       until it fires. No polling loop in the session. The job:
       - waits for the first run to appear, bounded: retry
         `gh run list --commit <merge-sha> --event pull_request --limit 100 --json databaseId,status,conclusion,headSha,workflowName`
         every 30 seconds for at most 15 minutes. No run by then → the job
         saves that result and ends; record it and STOP with an
         operator-only ask (CI never started);
       - then `gh run watch <run-id> --exit-status` per run, each result
         saved to a file, and lists the runs once more at the end, saving
         that list too. If the final list holds a run not yet completed,
         it watches that run too and lists again.
5. [ ] **Green** means the final run list (`--limit 100`: fewer than 100
       entries, or it is not the whole list — list again with a higher
       limit) holds at least one `pull_request` run for head SHA
       `<merge-sha>`, and every run in it concluded `success`. An empty run
       list is not green: it means CI has not started yet. Record the run URLs with
       `work event ci-candidate --note "<run URLs>: success at <merge-sha>"`.
       Then prove CI tested exactly the candidate: read the PR's base SHA
       (`gh pr view <pr> --json baseRefOid`) and
       `work verify candidate-merge-tree -- sh -c 'test "$(git merge-tree --write-tree <base-sha> <merge-sha>)" = "$(git rev-parse <merge-sha>^{tree})"'`.
       A differing tree means CI tested something else: STOP with an
       operator-only ask.
6. [ ] **Code scanning** (CodeQL), before the tag and before anything
       reaches `main` on the mirror. Read the alerts on the candidate PR's
       analysis
       (`gh api --paginate "repos/<owner>/<repo>/code-scanning/alerts?ref=refs/pull/<pr>/head&state=open"`).
       An empty alert list proves nothing until an analysis exists for this
       candidate: first require one in
       `gh api --paginate "repos/<owner>/<repo>/code-scanning/analyses?ref=refs/pull/<pr>/head"`
       created after the candidate push (default-setup CodeQL does not run
       as a `pull_request` workflow, so item 4 may not have waited for it;
       wait for it the same way). A 403 means the token lacks code-scanning
       read: an operator-only ask, never a skip. Only a 404 saying code
       scanning is not enabled counts as "not configured": record that in
       `github-ci-candidate.md` and go on.
       Otherwise triage every alert the analysis reports into
       `codeql-triage.md` (`work artifact`), each marked **new** (not open
       on the mirror's `main`, from
       `gh api --paginate "repos/<owner>/<repo>/code-scanning/alerts?ref=refs/heads/main&state=open"`)
       or **pre-existing**: the sink, the tainted source, and **who can
       actually reach that code path**, judged against the project's
       documented trust boundaries (its project info, from
       `work read-project-info`: who can reach the deployment, and what
       each caller can send). No documented boundaries → every caller is a
       non-operator. Probe a confinement claim rather than reading it off
       the code.
       - Any **new** alert a non-operator can reach with real impact → STOP
         with an operator-only ask before the tag and the `main` push, with
         `codeql-triage.md` attached.
       - Otherwise (every new alert operator-only or a false positive) →
         proceed without asking. Draft a follow-up issue at the end of
         `codeql-triage.md` covering those alerts and every pre-existing
         alert a non-operator can reach; pre-existing alerts never stop the
         release. `lmer-signal post` the draft to the supervising uber
         lmer, who files it.
       - Dismissing an alert needs the operator's explicit authorization,
         every time. Dismissal comments are public: self-contained, at most
         280 characters, no internal names, hosts, issue or MR numbers or
         run paths; run each through `lmer-release-guard scan-text` (0
         hits) before sending, and read each alert back afterwards.
7. [ ] **Red** — read the failing jobs (`gh run view <run-id> --log-failed`)
       and diagnose before anything else:
       - **Suspected flaky** (a timing or load failure in a test that
         passes locally): re-run the failed jobs ONCE on the same SHA
         (`gh run rerun <run-id> --failed`) without asking, record it
         (`work event ci-candidate-rerun --note "<run URL>"`), `lmer-signal
         post` that you did and why, and go back to item 4. ONCE is per
         candidate, across sessions: a `ci-candidate-rerun` event already in
         the run's `events.jsonl` (the run dir is in `work state`), or any
         run for the candidate with `attempt` above 1
         (`gh run view <run-id> --json attempt`, which survives a session
         that died before recording the event), means the rerun is spent,
         and this red is classified below.
       - **Test-only fix**, a fact about paths, not a judgement: every path
         in the fix's
         `git diff --name-only --no-renames origin/prep-release...HEAD` (a
         moved file shows both of its paths) is under `tests/`, or under a
         test root the project info names as one. Do not wait for the operator.
         Adopt, never duplicate: a `ci-candidate-fix` event in the run's
         `events.jsonl`, or an open MR from a `fix/release-ci-v<X.Y.Z>-*`
         branch, means the fix already exists — go to its merge check
         below. Otherwise branch off `origin/prep-release`
         (`fix/release-ci-v<X.Y.Z>-<topic>`), fix it, `bin/gate-commit`,
         and check the path predicate on the commit before anything is
         pushed; a path outside the test roots makes it the case below,
         and the branch stays local. Then `bin/gate-push`, and open an MR
         into `prep-release` — never `main`. Record it at once
         (`work event ci-candidate-fix --note "<MR URL>"`), add it to
         `github-ci-candidate.md`, and `lmer-signal post` the MR to the
         supervising uber lmer to coordinate its review. Then
         `work commit` and end the session; the scheduled relaunch checks
         the MR. The release holds:
         no tag, no further push to the mirror. The merge SHA is
         write-once, so this run can never tag: once the fix MR has
         MERGED, end the run with
         `work release abort --reason "superseded: GitHub CI red at <merge-sha>; test-only fix <MR URL> merged"`
         and report it — a fresh release run re-enters leg 1 idempotently
         and releases the same version from a new release merge. Until the
         fix MR merges, a follow-up session finds it open and exits; it
         never aborts early.
       - **Any fix outside test code** (a path outside the test roots):
         STOP with an operator-only ask carrying the diagnosis and the
         failing job URL. Change nothing.

### Step 3 — `leg2-create-tag` — the SSH-signed tag, at exactly the merge SHA

Only after step 2a found nothing and step 2b is green with its code-scanning
check passed.

The tag name is `release.tag_prefix` + the version re-read in step 1 at the
merge SHA — and Phase 0.5 pins `tag_prefix` to exactly `"v"`, so the name is
always `v<version>` (the kernel's `work release record tag` accepts nothing
else) — never a name remembered from leg 1.

- [ ] Idempotency check FIRST — does the tag already exist? Check locally
      and on both remotes (`git ls-remote origin refs/tags/<tag>`,
      `git ls-remote github refs/tags/<tag>` — the `github` one carrying the
      credential prefix in its own shell invocation). If it exists:
      - pointing at exactly the recorded merge SHA AND carrying a valid
        signature (verification below) → creation is DONE; record
        `work release record tag <tag> --sha <merge-sha>` if not yet
        recorded and continue to step 4.
      - pointing ANYWHERE else, or unsigned, or the signature does not
        verify → HARD ERROR. Never re-point, never re-sign, never delete.
        Record a critical error naming the tag, the SHA it points at, and
        the recorded merge SHA; `work commit`; end the session. (The burned
        version runbook in the resume section is the human's repair path.)
- [ ] Create the tag, signed with the provisioned key:

      ```
      git -c gpg.format=ssh -c user.signingkey=/release-signing-key \
          tag -s <tag> <merge-sha> -m "Release <version>"
      ```
- [ ] Verify before recording — the signature against the provisioned key's
      public half, and the target commit against the recorded merge SHA:
      `echo "* $(ssh-keygen -y -f /release-signing-key)" > /tmp/allowed-signers`
      then
      `git -c gpg.format=ssh -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=/tmp/allowed-signers tag -v <tag>`
      and `git rev-parse <tag>^{commit}` must print the merge SHA.
- [ ] Record it: `work release record tag <tag> --sha <merge-sha>`. The
      recorder itself hard-stops unless the SHA equals the recorded merge
      SHA and the name is exactly `v<version>` — a refusal here means the
      tag is wrong: treat it as the hard error above, do not argue with it.

### Step 4 — `leg2-push-github-main` / `leg2-push-github-tag` — GitHub: `main` first, THEN the tag

The ordering is load-bearing: the tag push triggers `release.yml`, and its
verify job asserts the tag equals GitHub `main` HEAD — `main` must land
before the trigger fires. Every push goes through `bin/gate-push`; the
session's push allow list already grants the mirror's branch push and its
tags (the `repo|refs/tags/*` grammar). A gate refusal is a provisioning gap
to report, never something to route around.

Before EACH of the two pushes, unless `leak_scan.public_mirror` is
`false`: a fresh credentialed `git fetch github`, then the guard for exactly
that object and ref —
`work verify prepush-guard-main -- lmer-release-guard prepush --config <resolved task.yaml> --version <X.Y.Z> --published github/main <merge-sha> refs/heads/main`
before `main`, and
`work verify prepush-guard-tag -- lmer-release-guard prepush --config <resolved task.yaml> --version <X.Y.Z> --published github/main <tag> refs/tags/<tag>`
before the tag. `GUARD FAIL` → do not push: STOP with an operator-only ask
quoting its FAIL lines. A non-zero exit without a `GUARD FAIL` line means
the guard did not run: a stop as well, never a pass.

- [ ] `main` first — skip when current: `git rev-parse github/main` already
      equal to the merge SHA → record the receipt (if absent) and move on.
      Otherwise, with local `main` checked out at the merge SHA, guard it,
      then:
      `bin/gate-push --remote github`
      A non-fast-forward rejection here is the divergence hard stop from
      step 2 (the mirror moved between check and push) — same contract, no
      retry, no force.
- [ ] Record: `work release record receipt github-main-push`
- [ ] Then the tag — skip when current:
      `git ls-remote github refs/tags/<tag>` already showing the recorded
      merge SHA → record the receipt (if absent) and move on. Otherwise
      guard it, then:
      `bin/gate-push --tag <tag> --remote github`
- [ ] Record: `work release record receipt github-tag-push`

Every `github`-remote command in this step — the `ls-remote` skip check and
both pushes — carries the ASKPASS prefix from the credentials section in
its own shell invocation; nothing persists from the commands before it.
`bin/gate-push` shells out to `git`, so the ASKPASS environment passes
through, but nothing else supplies the credential. A
rejected push (a 403 from a missing or read-only credential, a protection
rule) is a hard error naming the credential requirement — never an
alternative push path, per HARD RULES. Note the ordering cost of getting
this wrong: the tag already exists locally by this point, and the tag is
immutable, so recovery is fixing the credential and re-entering the leg,
never re-tagging.

### Step 5 — `leg2-poll-actions` — the GitHub Actions release run

`gh` takes the same PAT from `GH_TOKEN`, supplied PER COMMAND like ASKPASS:
every `gh` command below runs in its own subshell that exports the token, so
it reaches that one command and never its command line. The `work` receipts
and `bin/gate-push` stay OUTSIDE these subshells — neither needs `GH_TOKEN`.
The same subshell sets `GH_REPO` to `release.github_target`, the URL exactly
as resolved in Phase 0.5: `gh` reads the owner and repository from that URL,
so it never guesses the mirror from this checkout's git remotes.

Find the `release.yml` run for the recorded merge SHA on the mirror, filtered
by workflow and head SHA — the recorded SHA, never "the latest run":
`( export GH_TOKEN="${LMER_RELEASE_GITHUB_TOKEN}"; export GH_REPO="<release.github_target>"; export GH_PROMPT_DISABLED=1; gh run list --workflow release.yml --commit <merge-sha> )`

- [ ] Already green for that SHA → skip the poll; make sure the receipts
      below exist (their URLs must name the run that actually uploaded) and
      move on.
- [ ] Otherwise poll the run to completion:
      `( export GH_TOKEN="${LMER_RELEASE_GITHUB_TOKEN}"; export GH_REPO="<release.github_target>"; export GH_PROMPT_DISABLED=1; gh run watch <run-id> )`,
      or a loop on
      `( export GH_TOKEN="${LMER_RELEASE_GITHUB_TOKEN}"; export GH_REPO="<release.github_target>"; export GH_PROMPT_DISABLED=1; gh run view <run-id> )`.
- [ ] Green → PyPI has the version (with attestations) and the GitHub
      Release exists. Record which run actually published:
      `work release record receipt actions-run --url <Actions run URL>`
      `work release record receipt pypi --url <PyPI release URL>`
      `--url` is required on both. On a re-run, record the run that
      UPLOADED, not merely the last green one — a failed-jobs-only re-run
      leaves `publish-pypi` untouched, so a later green run may have
      uploaded nothing.
- [ ] Red, with refs current → re-dispatch the FAILED JOBS ONLY via the
      API, keyed on the recorded SHA:
      `( export GH_TOKEN="${LMER_RELEASE_GITHUB_TOKEN}"; export GH_REPO="<release.github_target>"; export GH_PROMPT_DISABLED=1; gh run rerun <run-id> --failed )`.
      The `--failed` is not optional. `release.yml` sets no
      `skip-existing`, so re-running a `publish-pypi` that already
      succeeded attempts an upload PyPI already holds; PyPI refuses it,
      and the run can never converge — leaving a published version with a
      missing GitHub Release and no way forward. `--failed` re-runs the
      failed jobs AND their dependents, which is right on both sides of a
      publish: red at `checks` or `build` re-runs everything downstream,
      and red only at `github-release` re-runs just that job, reusing the
      original run's `dist` artifact (which is why that job's download
      step passes `github-token` and `run-id`). NEVER re-tag to
      retrigger — the tag is immutable: never deleted, never re-pointed,
      never re-signed. Then poll again. A run that cannot be re-run is the
      fail-loudly path below.
- [ ] Red at **Publish to PyPI**, with PyPI reporting the version already
      exists → this is NOT an agent decision, and re-running cannot clear
      it. PyPI's refusal to accept a version it already holds IS the
      version-reuse gate; there is no variable to set and no override.
      Report it to the human with the Actions run URL via the fail-loudly
      path below. Recovery means cutting a new version — never
      republishing over the spent one.
- [ ] Still red → FAIL LOUDLY with the Actions run URL:
      `work state set --stop-reason=critical_error --critical-error
      '{"summary": "GitHub release run red", "detail": "<Actions run URL>"}'`
      then `work commit` and end the session. What to do about the red run
      is the human's call — the single API re-dispatch above is the only
      automatic remediation this leg attempts. Nothing has been published
      internally yet — that is the point of the ordering.

### Step 6 — `leg2-push-gitlab-tag` — the GitLab tag, LAST

Only after the GitHub run is green. Public-before-internal is deliberate: a
GitHub failure leaves no half-released internal state.

- [ ] Skip when current: `git ls-remote origin refs/tags/<tag>` already
      showing the recorded merge SHA → record the receipt (if absent) and
      move on. A GitLab tag at any OTHER SHA is the tag hard error from
      step 3 — never re-point it.
- [ ] Push: `bin/gate-push --tag <tag> --remote origin`
      GitLab's existing tag pipeline publishes the internal package
      registry and container image from there.
- [ ] Record: `work release record receipt gitlab-tag-push`

### Step 7 — `leg2-dep-refresh` — open the next cycle's dependency refresh

The release has shipped; `prep-release` now carries exactly what was
released, and the next cycle starts here. Refreshing dependencies NOW rather
than during leg 1 is the whole point: the refresh gets a full development
cycle of soak before it ships, where a relock landed alongside the version
bump would ship the same day it was resolved. Leg 1's rule that a `uv.lock`
diff beyond the project's own version line is a stop-and-ask stays exactly
as it is — this step is where dependency movement is allowed to happen.

This step never blocks the release: the release is already out. It opens an
MR and completes; a human merges it whenever they merge it.

- [ ] No `dep_refresh` parameter (Phase 0.5) → this repository has not opted
      in. Record `work release record receipt dep-refresh --note "no
      dep_refresh parameter declared"` and go to step 8. Do NOT infer a
      command from the repository tree.
- [ ] Adoption, never re-creation — FIRST, before creating anything. Check
      `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/dep-refresh-<X.Y.Z>` and for an open
      MR titled `dep refresh after <X.Y.Z>`. An open MR means a previous
      session died between opening it and recording the receipt: ADOPT it —
      record `work release record receipt dep-refresh --url <its URL>` and
      go to step 8. A remote branch with no MR means the push landed and the
      MR did not: fetch it, check it out, and pick up at the "Push and open
      the MR" bullet. NEVER open a second MR and NEVER force-push over it.
- [ ] Branch off the freshly released `prep-release`:
      `git fetch origin prep-release`,
      `git switch -c dep-refresh-<X.Y.Z> origin/prep-release`. Re-entry in
      the same container already has the local branch, so `-c` errors —
      `git switch dep-refresh-<X.Y.Z>` onto it instead, and reset it to
      `origin/prep-release` only if it carries no refresh yet.
- [ ] Run the declared `dep_refresh` command verbatim, from the repository
      root, through `work verify dep-refresh -- <command>` so the run
      carries a receipt of what it ran and what that produced. `work verify`
      mirrors the command's exit code: NON-ZERO means the refresh did not
      run to completion (an unreachable index, a missing target, a `make`
      target dying on its first line) and the tree may well still be clean —
      that is NOT "no dependency changes". Record `work release record
      receipt dep-refresh --note "refresh command failed — <one-line
      summary>"` and go to step 8, leaving no branch pushed.
- [ ] Only on a zero exit, read `git status --porcelain`. EMPTY means every
      dependency was already at the newest version its declared ranges allow
      — a real outcome, not a failure. Record `work release record receipt
      dep-refresh --note "no dependency changes"` and go to step 8.
- [ ] Otherwise stage what the refresh produced, then gate it. Stage every
      path `git status --porcelain` listed — `bin/gate-commit` commits the
      index and stages nothing, and its git-status check FAILS on any
      unstaged or untracked path, so an unstaged lockfile turns into a red
      gate that never ran the suite. A dependency refresh that breaks the
      suite is not a refresh: `bin/gate-commit -m "dep refresh after
      <X.Y.Z>"`. A RED gate is NOT a hard stop for the release — the release
      already shipped — but it IS a stop for this step: record `work release
      record receipt dep-refresh --note "refresh failed the gate — <one-line
      summary>"`, leave the branch unpushed, and say so in the retro. Never
      commit around a failing gate to get the MR open.
- [ ] Push with `bin/gate-push` (no arguments — it pushes the current branch
      to `origin`) and open the MR targeting `prep-release` — NEVER `main`.
      Title it `dep refresh after <X.Y.Z>`. Say in the description which
      packages moved and that the diff is lockfiles only.
- [ ] Record: `work release record receipt dep-refresh --url <MR URL>`

### Step 8 — receipts complete, run complete

- [ ] `work release status` must show every field recorded and nothing left
      to advance. Fill any receipt gap NOW (`--note` that it was recorded
      after the fact) — an unreceipted push is invisible to the resume
      derivation.
- [ ] Proceed to "Closing out the run" (retro, goal assessment, completion
      state, `work commit`).

### The idempotency ladder (re-entry map)

Leg 2 may be entered any number of times — watch death, container reap,
scheduled relaunch. Every re-entry converges because every step compares its
target to the recorded merge SHA before acting:

| Observed state | Action |
| --- | --- |
| Merge SHA already recorded | Re-record with the freshly re-read version — the mismatch check runs every time |
| No tag yet, a `ci-candidate-fix` event recorded | Checked BEFORE the next row. Fix MR merged → `work release abort` as superseded; still open → nothing to advance, exit |
| No tag yet | Re-run step 2a's leak scan (cheap) and step 2b from its first missing item: candidate branch already at the merge SHA → skip the push; open draft PR → adopt it; CI already green for the merge SHA → skip the wait; a `ci-candidate-rerun` event → the rerun is spent |
| Tag exists at the recorded merge SHA, signature valid | Skip creation; ensure the tag record exists |
| Tag exists anywhere else, or unsigned/invalid | HARD ERROR — never re-point, never re-sign, never delete |
| GitHub `main` already at the merge SHA | Skip the push; ensure the receipt exists |
| GitHub tag ref already at the merge SHA | Skip the push; ensure the receipt exists |
| Actions run green for the recorded SHA | Skip the poll; receipts must name the run that uploaded |
| Actions run red, refs current | Re-dispatch the FAILED JOBS ONLY via the API (`gh run rerun <run-id> --failed`), keyed on the recorded SHA — never re-tag, never a full re-run |
| GitLab tag already at the merge SHA | Skip the push; ensure the receipt exists |
| GitLab tag pushed, no `dep-refresh` receipt | Run step 7 — it is idempotent: an existing open `dep-refresh-<X.Y.Z>` MR is ADOPTED (record its URL as the receipt), never a second one |
| Every receipt recorded | Nothing to advance — close out the run |
